# Article: Local Engineering

## Verification

- Every role except the specifier runs the full suite -- `pytest` (unit and
  acceptance) -- before any handoff, and fixes what it broke.
- The architect, hardender, and QA additionally run `ruff check .`, `mypy src`,
  and the property tests (`pytest -m property`) when the project has them, and
  fix any failures before handing off.
- No handoff on a red suite. If you cannot get it green, hand back to the
  previous role with the failing test name and the reason -- do not pass a
  broken slice forward and do not delete or skip a test to make it pass.

## Acceptance scenarios

- Every scenario in `features/` has a step definition in
  `tests/acceptance/steps/`. An undefined step is a failing build, not a
  pending test.
- Never edit a `.feature` file to make a failing scenario pass. If the scenario
  is wrong, hand it back to the specifier with the scenario name and why.
- `pytest` runs unit tests and `pytest-bdd` scenarios together. There is one
  runner; do not add a second.

## Test discipline

- Tests are hermetic: no live HTTP, no real browser launch, no real worker
  node, no real sleeps (retry backoff takes an injectable clock), no dependence
  on wall-clock time or test ordering.
- Playwright and Selenium are faked at the driver boundary. A test that would
  fail on a machine without those browsers installed is a broken test.
- New behavior arrives with a test. A bug fix arrives with the test that would
  have caught it.
- Fixtures live in `fixtures/` and are committed. Do not download a page at test
  time to create one; save it once, deliberately, and check it in.

## Commits

- Small commits, one logical change each, present-tense subject line.
- Commit before handing off, so the next role's worktree sees your work.
